title: 'fork-tree: do not overflow the stack on deep trees'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |
    Issue: a `ForkTree` gains one level for every node imported on top of another, until an
    ancestor is finalized. Cloning, comparing, printing, encoding, decoding and dropping the
    tree were derived, so each used one stack frame per level. When finality stalled on Paseo
    the BABE epoch tree grew by one level per epoch, and the clone that BABE takes at every
    epoch boundary overflowed the 2 MiB worker stack, crashing the node (#12062).

    Solution: the roots and the children of a node are now held in a private `Nodes` wrapper,
    which implements these operations over the pre-order listing of the nodes with a
    heap-allocated stack. Their stack usage no longer depends on the depth of the tree. The
    encoding and the public API are unchanged.

crates:
- name: fork-tree
  bump: patch
